From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:08:07 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 36/63] hjviewer: wake-on-LAN: the app wakes a PC of the
 account that is off
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

In an account build a PC of the accessible devices tab that is off can be
woken from the app, through hjv-api and the relay on the PC's network (design
v3 1-9, 8-5). An iPhone does it as any other device: the requests are
hjv-api's, over HTTPS and signed as its other account requests, not the LAN
packet of the Discovered tab's "WOL". The screens are in hjviewer_account.dart;
their texts and decisions and the steps of a wake (HjvWaking, which gets the
app around it handed in, so that the tests run it) are in
hjviewer_account_texts.dart; beside them:

- peer_card.dart (D11): connectInPeerTab first asks hjvOfferWake. A PC of
  that tab whose dot is off gets "이 PC는 꺼져 있습니다. 켤까요?" with [켜기]
  and [닫기], and the connection the user asked for (the remote screen, file
  transfer, the camera, ...) goes on only once the wake brought the PC up. It
  then goes on with the app's own context, which the hook's last argument
  takes: the card may have been rebuilt by then. Any other card, tab or build
  goes on at once. "Terminal (Run as administrator)" leaves a mark for the
  session that follows it at once (IS_TERMINAL_ADMIN); the offer puts it aside
  until the connection goes on, so that no other terminal takes it.
- the cards' menu (D5, hjvDeviceMenuItems): "켜기", above "내 목록에서 빼기".
- src/hjviewer_account/api.rs: the device list the app keeps (hjv-my-devices)
  also keeps hjv-api's verdict of each PC (hjv.wol of /api/peers: ok, warn, no
  or unknown), and main_get_common answers "hjv-wol:<PC ID>" with it. The Dart
  Peer drops hjv, and asking hjv-api whenever a menu opens would make every
  menu wait for the network. A verdict that is no string counts as none: the
  PC stays in the list, and with it the proofs to it.

"켜기" and the offer are for a PC that is off, unless the verdict is no (no
wired card, or an account without a relay) or there is none (an hjv-api
without the wake API).

A wake is POST /api/hjv/devices/{id}/wake and then the progress dialog, which
every two seconds asks GET /api/hjv/wake/{job} and looks at the dot of the
PC's card, for five minutes at most. It says what design v3 8-5's table says:

- queued: "NAS(<the relay's name>)에 켜기 요청을 전달하는 중…", the name alone
  when it says NAS itself, as the names hjv-api makes up do ("NAS KZDV");
- sent: "NAS가 켜기 신호를 보냈습니다. PC가 켜지기를 기다립니다…", and only
  then: before hjv-api answered the dialog says "요청하는 중…";
- confirmed, or the dot turned green: "켜졌습니다. 연결합니다…";
- not confirmed, or five minutes over: "5분 안에 켜진 것을 확인하지
  못했습니다.", hjv-api's hints and [다시 켜기];
- failed: the text of no_result and not_active, no_iface, send_failed (and
  partial without a packet), rate_limited, expired (with [다시 켜기], which
  the limit of a wake a minute lets through by then); any other code, about
  which the relay and hjv-api disagree, is shown as it is;
- no job: no_relay, with [모든 중계기로 시도] and "같은 네트워크인지 확인되지
  않아 켜질지 알 수 없습니다." only when hjv-api says that trying all relays
  may help (any_relay), which then asks again with {"any_relay":true};
  relay_offline with "마지막 연결: 10/20 14:03" when the relay ever asked;
  no_wol_info; 429; a PC that left the list (404); and hjv-api's own text for
  what the app has none for (a clock off, no command key);
- online: "이미 켜져 있습니다.", and the app connects as after a wake.

hjv-api's hints (what may keep the PC from waking, in its words) wait behind
[준비 안내 보기] while the wake is under way or failed, and show at once when
it was not confirmed.

Once the PC is on, the device list comes again and the app connects by itself
while the dialog has been open since the request. It first waits for hbbs to
have the PC, asking it every two seconds for 30 seconds at most: the PC
registers there on a schedule of its own, and a connection before that finds
it offline. Settings → Account has "PC를 켠 뒤 바로 연결" (on; shown when the
account has a PC that can be woken): off, the dialog says "켜졌습니다." with
[연결]. Closing the dialog does not end the wake: when the PC comes up, the
dialog comes again with [연결] (a phone that is in a session by then gets a
toast), and a wake that failed is told in a toast. While [연결] waits, a PC
whose dot was green and is off again reads "PC가 다시 절전에 들어갔습니다."
with [다시 켜기]. A new wake of a PC ends the one before.

Outside an account build hjvOfferWake returns at once and the menu and the
settings get nothing, as before.

Tests: test/hjviewer_account_test.dart (pure, ci.sh): which PCs get "켜기";
every row of 8-5's table from hjv-api's answers as it sends them (the 202 of
a new job and of one under way, each job state and error, each refusal with
its fields, nginx's 429, an answer that is no JSON), with the buttons of each
state, and that nothing before sent says that the signal went out; the relay's
name; a job's ID that is not 32 hex digits is no job; a poll without an answer
leaves the job as it was, and a job that is gone or a login that ended ends
the wake; the job is followed every two seconds to its end, by the card's dot
when that is first, for five minutes at most though no poll gets an answer,
and no longer once another request took over; the wait for hbbs; the PC that
went back to sleep; no braces; and whole wakes against a scripted app (hjv-api's
answers, the card's dot by a clock the waits move, the user at the dialog):
the texts the dialog draws up to the connection the app starts by itself, the
dialog closed and coming again with [연결], or a toast on a phone in a session
and for a wake that failed, the setting off, the PC asleep again and [다시
켜기], [모든 중계기로 시도], a PC on already, a request started again, and a
new wake ending the one before. test/hjviewer_account_widgets_test.dart (needs
the generated bridge): the offer's two buttons, and the progress dialog's
buttons, hints and progress bar for each state. src/hjviewer_account/api.rs:
the kept list has the verdict, and "hjv-wol:<PC ID>" answers it, "" for a PC
without one; a verdict that is no string keeps the PC (ctl.rs: its tests' list
has the new field).

Known limits: a card's dot is behind for a moment after the app started or
came back, so a tap in that moment offers to wake a PC that is on; [켜기]
then finds it on and connects. A PC that hbbs does not have 30 seconds after
hjv-api saw it, or that went to sleep less than 30 seconds ago (hjv-api still
calls it on), is connected to all the same and found offline. "PC가 다시
절전에 들어갔습니다." needs the dot to have been green, so it comes only
while [연결] waits. A PC whose verdict is no has no "켜기", and the app tells
why only by the card's note from hjv-api. A result the relay sends after
hjv-api gave up on it (no_result) is not followed. Not tried on an iPhone or a
Windows PC yet.

(Design v3, work package W4: patch 0036.)

